WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.790 align:start position:0%
 
good afternoon everyone  on behalf of the department of the  interior  i'm very happy to welcome you here to  lake mead  and to say thank you  vice president harris and members of the  nevada delegation for being here today  to witness the effects of climate change  firsthand  this morning we heard directly from our  experts about the effects of climate  change and of the drought on our water  supplies  we heard  as well that we have  tools in place to help respond to the  the drought and help make sure our  communities can remain resilient to  climate change  we are very concerned about the  conditions that may be facing us in the  future  we know that it is important for our  generation to take on the task of being  able to address what may come next and  to develop the next round of agreements  that will help us get through  so that we can continue to thrive  we have tools available to conserve  water and plan for droughts  but we need to do more  the buildback better agenda  will help us  to make sure that we have those tools it  will it contains the valuable  pieces that will let us do even more and  more and more  thank you again vice president harris  and  we look forward to  continuing to work with you close  closely we appreciate the strong support  that we have from congress as well and  i'm happy to introduce representative  titus  well thank you very much and thank you  to the department of interior and its  great representatives who work on this  i'm so honored to be able to show the  natural beauty but also the results of  climate change to our vice president  thank you so much for being here you  know several things are going on at once  we're in the fastest growing cities in  the country we have two and about 2.25  million people and 40 million visitors  we're also the warmest community in the  country and third we have the smallest  allocation of water from the colorado  river  nonetheless we have been great stewards  and we have reduced our consumption by  45 percent but it's going down down down  even further and that's why  the provisions that address water  conservation and climate change  generally that are in the buildback  better bill and the infrastructure bill  are so critical this is our moment you  hear the existential argument look  around at this bathtub ring it's very  clear so thank you madam vice president  for being here and taking this message  back to  [Music]  washington well good afternoon i'm  stephen horsford and i'm proud to  represent nevada's fourth district  and to join with my colleagues to  welcome the vice president back to  nevada and to be able to show her first  hand  the effects of the drought and the  implications of climate change you know  growing up  here in las vegas  i've had the opportunity to enjoy lake  mead  as a as a kid growing up  and now as an adult bringing my own  children out here to fish this summer i  was sharing that with the vice president  but we have built  a  desert in the middle  we have built an oasis in the middle of  a desert and las vegas really has relied  on the water from lake mead that  unfortunately has dropped uh  considerably over the last decades  because of the effects of climate change  and that is why  we support and we know congress must act  to pass both the bipartisan  infrastructure bill as well as the build  back better act and its provisions to  protect and address the needs of our  environment and  climate change so we look forward to  continuing to work with your leadership  madam vice president and of course the  president and thank you for taking the  time to come and see this firsthand and  working with our entire delegation in  nevada  climate change is not  a partisan issue this is an issue that  democrats republicans and independents  understand must be addressed  and now is the time for us to act so  thank you madam vice president and i'll  turn it over to congresswoman susie lee  good afternoon it is my honor to welcome  the vice president to congressional  district 3 on the shores of lake mead  for 86 years lake mead has provided the  true promise that it is to our community  not only is it our recreation outlet for  many not just nevadans but tourists from  across the world it also is a source of  hydropower and a source of our water  and water is life and when we look at  lake mead it represents the lifeblood of  this community and that's why the steps  that all of the incredible staff that  gave us briefings today  doing on conservation have been so  incredibly important and but the work  that we are doing in congress led by the  vice president and president with the  bipartisan infrastructure package which  includes my water recycling bill which  will mean that we reserve water for milk  thousands of families in southern nevada  for years to come  but not only that the build back better  agenda will take on climate change head  on  and ultimately when we look to the  future that's the type of leadership we  need and so  i am proud to work alongside this  administration  to you know we say climate change is not  tomorrow it's today and when you look at  the ring here in lake mead we all  understand far too well what it means  and so with that it's my honor to  introduce madam vice president kamala  harris  congresswoman titus thank you for the  warm welcome and for your many years of  leadership on this issue in the united  states congress uh  congressman hortsford you so eloquently  talked about the the intergenerational  impact of this issue and will continue  to be an intergenerational impact and  i've spent time with you in your  district and know of your long-standing  leadership on this issue even before you  entered congress and congresswoman lee  thank you for the warm welcome to your  district and for your leadership and and  the invitation to join you  um here today  so this is  a fundamental issue which is the issue  of water  literally the issue of water as the  congress members have mentioned  every living thing  depends and requires on water and its  existence and its prevalence  and what we are seeing in this nation  and around the world is that this  climate crisis  fueled by in  many if not most ways by human behaviors  has resulted in the moment where we're  looking at this beautiful american  landmark  the product of a lot of thought over  generations about how we can supply  in particular the states of nevada  arizona california and including mexico  with the water that 25  25 million people rely on  this is such a fundamental issue  and so when we talk about the buildback  better agenda it is an agenda let's  break it down what is buildback better  what does it build better agenda it's  about climate  it's about families  and it's about jobs  and so and it's about health  and all of these issues are present when  we look at the issues that are impacting  lake mead as an example of what is  happening around the country  not to mention the fact that right now  all of all the western states  90 percent  of the western states are facing some  level of drought  so when we look at what's happening here  we know this is about this lake but it  is about a region it is about our nation  the build back better agenda  is thoughtful and forsightful and it  really is transformational in the way it  will address these issues so on the  issue of the drought the infrastructure  the bipartisan infra infrastructure deal  combined with the buildback better  agenda is about what we need to do to  invest in things like water recycling  and reuse  what we can do in terms of water  desalination  what we can do in terms of  implementation of drought contingency  plans  this is about thinking ahead recognizing  where we are and where we're headed  if we don't address these issues with a  sense of urgency  understanding this is literally about  life  on the issue of jobs the bipartisan  infrastructure deal as was mentioned  during our our tour  will be about good union jobs the work  that needs to be done to help us build  up resiliency adaptation and also combat  the climate crisis that's about the jobs  that are about  pipefitters electricians  plumbers these are good union jobs that  are about restoring  our  basic infrastructure upon which we rely  for these fundamental needs  on the issue of climate well the build  back better agenda will help us tackle  the climate crisis with investments in  clean energy and electric vehicles and  so we can reduce emissions and why do we  need to reduce emissions because that is  part of what is contributing  to these drought conditions  so this is the work that we are talking  about with these two bills that are  before congress  and it is critical that we as a nation  understand that we have within our hands  within our possession the ability to  actually change the course of where  we're headed  just look out at this lake  look at  where the water has receded  over just the last 20 years  that space is larger than the height of  the statue of liberty in just the last  20 years  this is where we're headed  so let's take on a sense of  responsibility with a sense of urgency  and do something about this  in a way that is about satisfying basic  needs which in this case right here is  25 million people  who are served by what happens at lake  mead  not to mention what we can do that is  about future generations  so let's get these bills passed let's  understand that right now we have a  moment  where we have the ability  to actually  benefit future generations and be  transformational  in a way that will not only  be about life but about  this beautiful planet and beautiful  places like lake mead thank you all

